<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lahaul</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lahul</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lahaul is a high mountain valley in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himachal-pradesh">Himachal Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, north of the Rohtang Pass, drained by the Chandra and Bhaga rivers. Its people combine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhist</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> traditions, and it lay on the trade routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kullu">Kullu</a> and Ladakh, whose kings disputed its control. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spiti">Spiti</a> it now forms Lahaul and Spiti district, and the Atal Tunnel, opened in 2020, links it to Kullu.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lahaul.jpg" alt="Lahaul" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This Photo was taken by Timothy A. Gonsalves. Feel free to use my photos, but please mention me as the author. … · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kardhang_Biling_Bhaga_Dhauladhar_Oct22_A7C_04645.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lahaul lies in the west of Lahaul and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spiti">Spiti</a> district, with Keylong as its main town, geologically in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a> and on the Tibetan Plateau. It is connected to Manali in the south through the Rohtang Pass. It has three valleys: the Chandra Valley, locally Rangloi, after the Chandra River; the Bhaga Valley, locally Ghar, after the Bhaga River; and the Chandra-Bhaga Valley, locally Pattan and further on the Chenab Valley, where the two rivers meet at Tandi. The Kunzum Pass, at 4,551 metres, connects it to Spiti in the east. Lahaul receives much more rainfall than Spiti.</p><p>The harsh conditions allow only scattered tufts of hardy grasses and shrubs to grow even below 4 km, and glacier lines are usually at about 5 km. Farmers grow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potatoes</a>, cabbages and green peas as cash crops.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Lahaul was under the influence of the kingdom of Ladakh until the early 17th century, though the rulers of Chamba and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kullu">Kullu</a> also vied for control. In the 17th century it came under the Rajas of Kullu, particularly Biddhi Singh (r. 1672 to 1688) and Man Singh (r. 1688 to 1719). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dogras">Dogra</a> forces captured it in the early 19th century, and after the Anglo-Sikh War of 1845-46 it came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">British Raj</a> as a waziri of the Kullu sub-division of the Kangra district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>.</p><p>Three hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagir">jagir</a> districts, Kolong, Gumrang and Gondhla, each headed by a Thakur, had pre-dated British rule, and the British made the Thakur of Kolong wazir of all Lahaul, a magistrate, police officer, jailer, revenue collector and forest officer in one. The last of the line was Thakur Pratap Chand, by the 1930s. The wazir's powers were reduced from 1941, and Lahaul and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spiti">Spiti</a> were joined as a district in 1960; in 1966 it became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himachal-pradesh">Himachal Pradesh</a>.</p><p>Trade was an important occupation beside agriculture and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pastoralism">pastoralism</a>. A major Central Asian route through Lahaul linked the plains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kullu">Kullu</a> valley with Ladakh and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarkand">Yarkand</a>, and Patseo, near Darcha, held an important annual wool mart in the colonial period. Moravian missionaries, among them Heinrich August Jäschke, ran a mission at Keylong from 1853 to 1940. They won few converts but introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potatoes</a>, turnips, lettuce, spinach and cauliflower, as well as knitting and newspapers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> gave Lahaul and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spiti">Spiti</a> district a population of 31,564, all rural. The Lahaulis are generally of Tibetan and Indo-Aryan descent, and the language of both Lahauli and Spiti Bhots is Bhoti, a Tibetic language. The family is the basic unit of kinship, headed by the senior male, the Yunda, and polyandry, once widely practised, has been dying out. Historic sites include the monastery and fort at Gondhla, the Triloknath temple, Khangsar palace and the Mrikula Devi temple.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lahaul and Spiti district” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kullu controlled trade routes to <b>Lahaul</b>, Spiti and Ladakh, and under Man Singh it reached its greatest extent.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kullu">Kingdom of Kullu</a></li></ul></section>
